\end{rem}
For the good-conditioned eigenvalue of the Hessian $\nabla^{2} f(x^{\star})$, every method such as gradient method, momentum method, Nesterov accelerated gradient method and artificially dissipating energy method has the good convergence rate shown by the experiment. However, for our artificially dissipating energy method, since there are trigonometric functions from~(\ref{eqn:sol}), we cannot propose the rigorous mathematic proof for the convergence rate. If everybody can propose a theoretical proof, it is very beautiful. Here, we propose a theoretical approximation for ill-conditioned case, that is, the direction with small eigenvalue $\lambda (\nabla^{2}f(x^{\star})) \ll L$. 
